 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Fashion is a major draw in museums, and from today it’s not just traditional institutions but outdoor ones as well. Collina Strada’s Hillary Taymour became the first fashion designer to present a runway show at Storm King. This was by invitation. “We felt that this was a collaboration that on both sides had such a passionate interest in ecology and sustainability and thoughtfulness,” said Storm King director Nora Lawrence, who believed that the interaction of clothing, sculpture, and landscape would “create something really incredible that’s bigger than anything separately.”</p><p>There really was magic in the air. The sun was out; there were dragonflies and drones, as well as an element of dissonance. Apart from a Dolly Parton song on the soundtrack, Taymour didn’t go country; rather, club music blasted in a meadow as models with mohawks and spiked hair traipsed by in ruffled sneakers the designer made for Converse. Spring at Collina Strada was about the three <em>r</em>’s times two: reduce, reuse, and recycle; and Rococo, riot grrrl, and “cool-girl Ren Faire.”</p><p>“I always try to go with a little bit of romance and beauty, but I always try to give it this grunge element,” Taymour said at a preview. The designer carried over some 18th-century loveliness (see the opening ruffled moiré cape) from the not-for-sale collection she presented at Copenhagen Fashion Week and introduced medieval (see the plaid printed sequined fabric that is a stand-in for chain mail) after watching Heath Ledger and Shannyn Sossamon in <em>A Knight’s Tale</em> on the flight back. “My whole goal is to make everything big and voluminous and as light as possible,” said Taymour, who wasn’t literal about her inspirations. She has a grip on fun and finances; based on what customers are buying, the designer showed a lot of skirts. The brand’s roomy pants had rosettes at each hip and were dip-dyed at the waist and hem. “We need to make clothes that people can wear every day—also there should be fantasy,” Taymour noted. Some of that was delivered with ruching, back drapes, and streamers. It’s becoming something of a Collina Strada signature to have fabric billowing out behind the models as they walk.</p><p>After the last look, Carly Rae Jepsen appeared in a pink dress and sang about Marie Antoinette. Taymour took her bow, and the models ran across the field and started dancing as Jepsen sang. Many in the audience joined in. Guests were then driven in golf carts to a picnic to reveal Collina Strada’s collaboration with Swedish interior company Layered. There were BonBon candies, a margarita fountain, princess torte, and many happy faces.</p><p>There’s been a lot of talk, and some grumbling, about show locations that aren’t central. The schedule mostly demands that they have to be, but should we only pay heed to fashion that comes to us? Collina Strada’s Storm King adventure was good for the industry because it shows fashion’s reach and impact outside the bubble. It also reminded us that fashion, even when it is concerned with artifice, can connect to nature in a positive rather than extractive way. Maybe country is the new punk?</p></div>",
